  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Edema associated with pregnancy varies from mild to severe pitting edema of the distal pelvic limbs, the ventral abdomen, mammary glands, and perineal subcutaneous tissues; sometimes accompanied with an increase in intrauterine amniotic/allantoic fluid accumulation.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Abnormal fluid accumulation in the fetus/neonate, hydrops fetalis, is a separate disorder, and not a comorbidity.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Edema in canine pregnancy has been referred to as “hydrops” by breeders; there is no evidence that the condition is comparable to hydrops in humans. The pathophysiology of the pregnancy edema syndrome in the dog has not yet been established. Hydrops allantois and hydrops amnii have been reported in mares and cows.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Edema can occur secondary to venous/lymphatic compression from an enlarged, gravid uterus.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "In humans, pregnancy edema can be normal (80%), or can be associated with preeclampsia, pregnancy toxemia, and eclampsia (proteinuric hypertension); a placental trigger is suspected (immunologic).",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "During normal pregnancy total body water increases both extracellularly and interstitially. There is also cumulative retention of sodium distributed between the maternal extracellular compartments and the fetus. Changes in factors governing renal sodium and water handling accompany alterations in local Starling forces whereby there is a moderate fall in interstitial fluid colloid osmotic pressure and a rise in capillary hydrostatic pressure, as well as changes in hydration of connective tissue.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SYSTEMS AFFECTED": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "canonical_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cardiovascular.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Endocrine/metabolic.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hemic/lymphatic/immune.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hepatobiliary.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Musculoskeletal.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Renal/urologic.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Reproductive.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "GENETICS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "GENETICS",
          "canonical_heading": "GENETICS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Heritability unknown, breed tendencies (see Signalment) support DNA sampling in confirmed cases for future genetic evaluation.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "INCIDENCE/PREVALENCE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "INCIDENCE/PREVALENCE",
          "canonical_heading": "INCIDENCE/PREVALENCE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Uncommon",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "GEOGRAPHIC DISTRIBUTION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "source_heading": "GEOGRAPHIC DISTRIBUTION",
          "canonical_heading": "GEOGRAPHIC DISTRIBUTION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "N/A",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SIGNALMENT": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Pregnancy edema occurs most commonly in bitches with very large litters in mid to late gestation. Many breeds have been affected,",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "most commonly reported are golden retrievers, Labrador retrievers, pugs, Doberman pinschers, English bulldogs and French bulldogs, and bullmastiffs.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "SIGNS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNS",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Swelling/pitting edema of pelvic limbs, paws, mammary glands, perineum, ventrum, vulva.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Marked abdominomegaly.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Marked and sudden weight gain.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Variable anorexia, nausea and lethargy; sometimes hemorrhagic gastroenterocolitis.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Possible systemic hypertension.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Possible increased allantoic/amniotic fluid volume.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Possible ascites.",
              "pdf_page": 1205,
              "printed_page": 1123,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "CAUSES": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "source_heading": "CAUSES",
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES",
          "content_blocks": [
